      <Graphics centerX="650" centerY="1200" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="zhang, PMN">|FRAME: L-1-phosphatidyl-inositols &quot;L-1-phosphatidyl-inositols&quot;| are glycerophospholipids that serve as a a minor component in the cytosolic side of eukaryotic cell membranes. The most common fatty acids of phosphatidyl-inositol are |FRAME: STEARIC_ACID| in the SN1 position and |FRAME: ARACHIDONIC_ACID| in the SN2 position.</Comment>
